course: dictionary
lesson: 8787
title: Words with a story
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can mean more than one thing, and a dictionary lists each meaning with a number. Shampoo is a noun and a verb. As a noun it is a liquid soap for washing hair, and it can also be a liquid for carpets, cars or pets. As a verb, to shampoo means to wash hair or a carpet with shampoo. «I shampoo my hair every morning» uses shampoo as a doing word, so it is a verb.
explain: Words also travel. Priya's grandma says, «Pyjamas, shampoo and bungalow are Hindi words, beta!» She is right: these words came into English from Hindi and Urdu during British rule in India. Shampoo first meant to press or massage, which is a different job from washing. The meaning changed over time, so always check the dictionary when a word surprises you.
explain: Adverts use numbers to sell. A shampoo advert said, «97% of women agreed their hair was shinier», but in tiny writing it added «of 34 women asked». Read the small print to see how big the group really was. Shops also make offers that look good. Shampoo at £3 a bottle on 3 for 2 costs £6 for three, which is £2 a bottle, but only if you really need all three.
fact: Shampoo is a noun for a liquid soap, and a verb meaning to wash with shampoo.
fact: Shampoo can be used for hair, carpets, cars and pets.
fact: Pyjamas, shampoo and bungalow came into English from Hindi and Urdu.
fact: «Shampoo» first meant to press or massage.
fact: Packaging is designed to sell, so read the label to see what is really inside.
exercise: Mum buys dog shampoo for Bruno's bath. What is shampoo here? | A small brush for combing fur | *A liquid for washing pets | A bowl for water | A toy for playing fetch
exercise: The advert said «97% of women agreed», and in tiny writing it said «of 34 women asked». How many women were asked? | *34 | 131 | 97 | 3
exercise: Which of these words came into English from Hindi or Urdu? | Tomato | Computer | *Pyjamas | Pizza
test: In «I shampoo my hair every morning», what kind of word is shampoo? | *Verb, a doing word | Adjective, a describing word | Adverb, a word about how | Noun, a naming word
test: Three bottles on 3 for 2 cost £6, but you only need one bottle at £3. How much extra do you spend because of the offer? | £6 | £1 | £2 | *£3
exam: Why should smart shoppers read the label rather than the box? | Because labels have pictures of bubbles | Because the box is always the cheapest | *Because packaging is designed to sell, while the label shows what is inside | Because boxes are always empty
